.TH BRK 2
.SH NAME
brk, sbrk, break \- change core allocation
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B "char *brk(addr)"
.PP
.B "char *sbrk(incr)"
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B brk
sets the system's idea of the lowest location not used by the program
(called the break) to
.IR addr .
Locations not less than
.I addr
and below the
stack pointer are not in the address space and will thus cause a memory
violation if accessed.
.PP
In the alternate function
.BR sbrk ,
.I incr
more bytes are added to the
program's data space and a pointer to the start of the new area is
returned.
.PP
When a program begins execution via
.BR exec (2)
the break is set at the
highest location defined by the program and data storage areas.
Ordinarily,
therefore, only programs with growing data areas need to use
these calls.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
exec(2), malloc(3), end(3)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Zero is returned if the break could be set; \-1 if the program requests
more memory than the system limit.
.SH ASSEMBLER
(break = 17.)
.TP
PDP-11:
.nf
sys break; addr
.fi
.TP
8086:
.nf
BX = 17
AX = addr
int 0Xf1
.fi
.PP
.B break
performs the function of
.BR brk .
The name of the routine differs
from that in C for historical reasons.
.sp
